1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,433
Bench Day is a reception board for a small community repair event.
This

2
00:00:03,433 --> 00:00:06,865
walkthrough uses fictional people and repairs in the running app.
Seven items are

3
00:00:06,865 --> 00:00:10,298
waiting, and three volunteers are available. The next batch turns
those separate lists

4
00:00:10,298 --> 00:00:13,730
into a shared plan.

5
00:00:14,430 --> 00:00:18,513
Each volunteer has skills, a shift, and a duty status. Rowan can
repair

6
00:00:18,513 --> 00:00:22,597
bicycles and electrical items. Alex handles bicycles, and Sam handles
textiles. The bike

7
00:00:22,597 --> 00:00:26,680
stand, electrical bench, and sewing machine each have one available
unit. Jo is

8
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:30,763
off duty.

9
00:00:31,463 --> 00:00:35,196
Giving the first bicycle to Rowan would leave Alex idle and the lamp

10
00:00:35,196 --> 00:00:38,928
waiting. In this example, that greedy baseline starts two repairs.
Bench Day assigns

11
00:00:38,928 --> 00:00:42,660
Alex to the bicycle, Rowan to the lamp, and Sam to the bag,

12
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:46,392
starting three. This is a software example, not measured field impact.
The safety

13
00:00:46,392 --> 00:00:50,124
hold keeps the kettle out.

14
00:00:50,824 --> 00:00:54,810
The operator reviews the batch with the volunteers before confirming
it. The app

15
00:00:54,810 --> 00:00:58,796
checks skills, shifts, closing time, and shared tools again. People
remain responsible for

16
00:00:58,796 --> 00:01:02,782
safety and for the estimates they enter.

17
00:01:03,482 --> 00:01:08,137
After confirmation, three repairs are active. The other bicycle waits
because its stand

18
00:01:08,137 --> 00:01:12,792
is in use. The jacket waits because Sam is working. Those resources
stay

19
00:01:12,792 --> 00:01:17,446
reserved until someone records an outcome, even if the estimated time
has passed.

20
00:01:18,146 --> 00:01:22,008
The event clock is manual. Here it moves from ten o clock to

21
00:01:22,008 --> 00:01:25,870
ten twenty. Reception should set it before check-in, dispatch, and
outcome recording. Advancing

22
00:01:25,870 --> 00:01:29,732
the clock does not mark any repair finished.

23
00:01:30,432 --> 00:01:34,253
Sam has finished the bag. The operator can record repaired, needs
parts, or

24
00:01:34,253 --> 00:01:38,074
not repaired. This example records repaired at ten twenty. That
explicit result releases

25
00:01:38,074 --> 00:01:41,895
Sam and the sewing machine.

26
00:01:42,595 --> 00:01:46,721
The proposal updates immediately. Sam can now take the jacket, while
the bicycle

27
00:01:46,721 --> 00:01:50,847
and electrical bench remain busy. The kettle is still on hold.
Explanations beside

28
00:01:50,847 --> 00:01:54,973
each waiting item show what is preventing its assignment.

29
00:01:55,673 --> 00:01:59,350
The repair log shows the bag, its volunteer, the start and finish
times,

30
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:03,027
and the recorded outcome. It can be printed for the event record. An

31
00:02:03,027 --> 00:02:06,704
estimate is never counted as a completed repair.

32
00:02:07,404 --> 00:02:11,169
Event data stays in this browser. A second tab is read-only. JSON
snapshots

33
00:02:11,169 --> 00:02:14,935
can be exported and validated on import. The cached app was also
checked

34
00:02:14,935 --> 00:02:18,700
with its local server stopped. Backups are not encrypted, so use
nicknames and

35
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:22,465
avoid sensitive details.

36
00:02:23,165 --> 00:02:27,434
Eight tests pass, including three hundred small cases compared with an
independent exhaustive

37
00:02:27,434 --> 00:02:31,704
oracle. A search-limit warning appears if the best batch is not
proven. Codex

38
00:02:31,704 --> 00:02:35,973
assisted the implementation, tests, and documentation. The next step
is testing these assumptions

39
00:02:35,973 --> 00:02:40,242
with a real event organizer.
